  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "thanjavur",
      "phase": "revival",
      "title": "The Pandyas take Thanjavur",
      "native": "தஞ்சாவூர்",
      "when": "c. 1216 to 1238 CE",
      "at": 8,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.6,
          10.6
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "For three centuries the Pandyas had mostly lived under the Cholas. Maravarman Sundara Pandya I, king from about 1216, turned the tables. Around 1219 he sacked Thanjavur and Uraiyur, the Chola capitals, and his inscriptions boast of burning palaces. The Hoysala king Narasimha II came to the Cholas’ aid, and the Pandya restored the Chola king as a subordinate.",
      "today": "Thanjavur, Tamil Nadu, around the great Chola temple of Rajaraja I."
    },
    {
      "place": "srirangam",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Roofs of gold",
      "native": "திருவரங்கம்",
      "when": "1251 to 1268 CE",
      "at": 24,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.6,
          10.6
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I was the greatest of the later Pandyas. He killed the Hoysala Someshvara near Kannanur, about 1262, and drove the Hoysalas from the Kaveri delta. His inscriptions say he covered the shrines of Srirangam and Chidambaram with gold, and he was remembered as ‘the king who roofed with gold’ (Ponmeynda Perumal).",
      "today": "Srirangam, Tiruchirappalli; its Ranganatha temple is among the largest working temples in the world."
    },
    {
      "place": "nellore",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Crowned at Nellore",
      "native": "நெல்லூர்",
      "when": "c. 1258 to 1264 CE",
      "at": 30,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          79,
          12.2
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "madurai",
          "to": "nellore",
          "traveller": "pandya-army",
          "style": "pandya",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Jatavarman marched north as no Pandya had before. He forced the Kadava chief Kopperunjinga to submit, took Kanchipuram, and beat the Kakatiya Ganapatideva and Telugu chiefs near Nellore, where he performed a victor’s anointing (virabhisheka). He also sent armies to Sri Lanka. For a few years the Pandyas held the east coast to the Telugu country.",
      "today": "Nellore, on the Penna river, Andhra Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "place": "yapahuwa",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "The Tooth Relic taken",
      "native": "යාපහුව",
      "lang": "si",
      "when": "c. 1284 CE",
      "at": 44,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          79.3,
          9.3
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "yapahuwa",
          "to": "madurai",
          "traveller": "pandya-army",
          "style": "pandya",
          "bend": 0.12
        }
      ],
      "text": "The Sri Lankan chronicle, the Culavamsa, says a Pandya general, Aryacakravarti, invaded during a famine, took the rock fortress of Yapahuwa and carried the Buddha’s Tooth Relic, the symbol of Sinhala kingship, to King Kulasekhara. Parakramabahu III later went to the Pandya court and won it back by diplomacy. The chronicle gives no exact year; about 1284 is usual.",
      "today": "Yapahuwa, Kurunegala District, Sri Lanka; the relic is now kept in Kandy."
    },
    {
      "place": "kayal",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Marco Polo in Ma’bar",
      "native": "காயல்",
      "when": "c. 1292 CE",
      "at": 51,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.3,
          9.4
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Marco Polo, sailing home from China about 1292, called this coast Ma’bar and praised its riches. He describes divers bringing up pearls, protected by charmers against sharks, kings dressed in little but jewels, and the busy port of Kayal. The Persian historian Wassaf says the Pandyas bought some 10,000 Arabian horses a year, for few lived long there.",
      "today": "Palayakayal, near Thoothukudi; the old harbour has silted up."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "A war of brothers",
      "native": "மதுரை",
      "when": "1268 to 1310 CE",
      "at": 58,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          9.9
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Maravarman Kulasekhara I ruled for over forty years, the height of Pandya wealth. Wassaf says he favoured Vira Pandya, a concubine’s son, over the queen’s son Sundara Pandya. In 1310, by Wassaf’s account, Sundara killed his father and seized Madurai, Vira fought back, and the brothers split the kingdom. Sundara is said to have sought Delhi’s help.",
      "today": "Madurai, Tamil Nadu, around the Meenakshi temple."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "Malik Kafur in the south",
      "native": "மதுரை",
      "when": "1311 CE",
      "at": 66,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.2,
          11.5
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "dorasamudra",
          "to": "madurai",
          "traveller": "kafur-army",
          "style": "delhi",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "From Dorasamudra, guided by the Hoysala king, Malik Kafur entered the Pandya country in 1311. He chased Vira Pandya through forests and rain without catching him, and sacked temples, among them one at a place Amir Khusrau calls Barmatpuri, often taken to be Chidambaram. He found Madurai abandoned by Sundara, and went home with vast treasure and hundreds of elephants.",
      "today": "The Nataraja temple at Chidambaram, Tamil Nadu."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "The Sultanate of Madurai",
      "native": "மதுரை சுல்தானகம்",
      "when": "1335 to c. 1378 CE",
      "at": 80,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.2
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Delhi’s armies returned in the 1320s, and Ma’bar became a province. In 1335 its governor, Jalaluddin Ahsan Khan, declared himself sultan at Madurai. Ibn Battuta, whose wife was Ahsan Khan’s daughter, came in 1344 under a later sultan and found a cruel court and a city struck by plague. Vijayanagara’s prince Kumara Kampana took Madurai in about 1371.",
      "today": "Madurai’s Goripalayam mosque holds tombs said to be those of two sultans."
    },
    {
      "place": "tenkasi",
      "phase": "after",
      "title": "The Pandyas of Tenkasi",
      "native": "தென்காசி",
      "when": "15th and 16th centuries CE",
      "at": 96,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.8,
          10
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Pandya princes lived on as local kings, under Vijayanagara and then the Nayakas of Madurai. In the 1440s Parakrama Pandya began the great Kasi Vishwanatha temple at Tenkasi, ‘the Kashi of the south’, by tradition after a dream. Later Tenkasi Pandyas, such as Ativira Rama, wrote Tamil poetry. The line fades in the 16th century.",
      "today": "Tenkasi, Tamil Nadu; the temple’s tall gateway tower was rebuilt in modern times."
    }
  ],
