  "people": {
    "heading": "Pandya kings",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "maravarman-sundara-i",
        "name": "Maravarman Sundara Pandya I",
        "native": "மாறவர்மன் சுந்தர பாண்டியன்",
        "life": "r. c. 1216 to 1238",
        "role": "The king who began the revival",
        "steps": [
          0
        ],
        "text": "He began the Pandya revival by beating the Cholas, whose vassals his family had been. His wars against Rajaraja III made Madurai the rising power of the south. His inscriptions call him the king who took the Chola country and gave it back, for the Hoysalas stepped in and the Chola king was restored as his subordinate."
      },
      {
        "id": "jatavarman-sundara-i",
        "name": "Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I",
        "native": "சடையவர்மன் சுந்தர பாண்டியன்",
        "life": "r. 1251 to c. 1268",
        "role": "The greatest of the later Pandyas",
        "steps": [
          1,
          2
        ],
        "text": "He fought the Hoysalas, the Kakatiyas, the Kadavas and Sri Lanka, and his inscriptions are found from Kanyakumari to Nellore. Pandya kings often ruled jointly, several at once, which may explain Marco Polo’s ‘five brother kings’ a generation later. Much of his wealth went to temples, above all Srirangam and Chidambaram."
      },
      {
        "id": "kulasekhara-i",
        "name": "Maravarman Kulasekhara I",
        "native": "மாறவர்மன் குலசேகர பாண்டியன்",
        "life": "r. 1268 to 1310",
        "role": "King at the height of Pandya trade",
        "steps": [
          3,
          4,
          5
        ],
        "text": "He ruled for over forty years, when Kayal’s harbour drew ships from Arabia and China. His general carried the Tooth Relic from Sri Lanka, and Persian writers knew him as Kales Dewar. He ended the last Chola king’s rule about 1279. His murder, by Wassaf’s account at the hands of a son, began the war that brought Malik Kafur."
      }
    ]
  }
